\title{Kernel Spectra of Diophantine Cones:\\
Equality Patterns, Defects, and Complete Realisation Criteria\\ for Ternary Conics}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
To a homogeneous Diophantine equation $F(x_1,\dots,x_n)=0$ we attach a finite invariant, its
\emph{kernel spectrum}: the set of equality patterns (set partitions of the index set) realised by the
coordinates of its non-negative integral solutions. The \emph{defect} of $F$ is the Bell number $B_n$,
the total number of patterns of an $n$-tuple, minus the cardinality of the spectrum. The central
computation is a complete realisation criterion for the three \emph{mixed} patterns of an arbitrary
ternary conic $Ax^2+By^2=Cz^2$: each criterion pairs a \emph{square condition}, a classical descent
obstruction, with a common \emph{degeneracy clause} $A+B\neq C$ asserting that the diagonal point
$(1,1,1)$ does not lie on the conic. Both arise from a single two-parameter descent lemma: for
$Q\neq 0$, the equation $Pu^2=Qv^2$ has a solution with $u\neq0$ and $u\neq v$ if and only if $PQ$ is a
perfect square and $P\neq Q$. Consequently, whenever $A+B=C$ all three mixed patterns vanish
simultaneously and the defect is at least $3$. Applying the criteria to the pencil $x^2+y^2=Cz^2$ we
prove that the defect is surjective onto $\{0,1,2,3,4\}$, witnessed by $C=50,1,8,2,3$, where $C=1$ is
the Pythagorean cone (defect $1$, missing pattern ``equal legs'', obstructed by the non-squareness of
$2$) and $C=3$ requires infinite descent at the prime $3$ to reach the maximal defect $4$. In higher
dimension a rigidity theorem valid for every leg count -- if $\sum_{i<k}x_i^2=y^2$ and the hypotenuse
equals a leg, all other legs vanish -- forces the hypotenuse-merged part of the spectrum to have
exactly $k+1$ elements for $k\ge2$ and kills six of the seven patterns missing from the spectrum of
$x^2+y^2+z^2=w^2$, which realises exactly $8$ of the $B_4=15$ patterns. The seventh is governed by the
criterion: the pattern ``all legs equal, hypotenuse apart'' is realised if and only if the leg count is
a perfect square other than $1$. For higher exponents we prove a $p$-th power analogue and exhibit a
cubic trichotomy showing the power and degeneracy obstructions to be logically independent.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The integral points of a homogeneous Diophantine equation are usually studied quantitatively. This
paper studies a qualitative shadow of that set: given a solution $(x_1,\dots,x_n)$, forget the values
and remember only which coordinates coincide. What remains is a set partition of $\{1,\dots,n\}$, and
one may ask which partitions arise.

For the Pythagorean cone $x^2+y^2=z^2$ there are five partitions of a three-element set, and four are
realised at once:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
pattern & witness \\
\midrule
all coordinates distinct & $(3,4,5)$\\
$x=z\neq y$ & $(7,0,7)$\\
$y=z\neq x$ & $(0,7,7)$\\
all coordinates equal & $(0,0,0)$\\
$x=y\neq z$ (equal legs) & \textbf{none}\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The last row is the statement that $2$ is not a perfect square: $x=y$ gives $2x^2=z^2$, forcing
$x=z=0$ and contradicting $x\neq z$. The cone therefore realises $4$ of $5$ patterns; its
\emph{defect} is $1$. Three questions organise the paper: is the defect computable in general (for
ternary conics, completely, Section~\ref{sec:conics}); is it a nontrivial invariant (it attains all
five possible values along one explicit pencil, Section~\ref{sec:surj}); and how does it behave in
higher dimension and at higher exponent (Sections~\ref{sec:higher} and~\ref{sec:fermat}).

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, ``number'' means a non-negative integer, and solutions are sought in $\NN$.

\begin{definition}[Equality pattern]
Let $t=(t_0,\dots,t_{n-1})$ be a tuple with entries in a set with decidable equality. Its \emph{kernel}
is the equivalence relation $i\sim j \iff t_i=t_j$ on indices, equivalently the partition of the index
set into level sets of $t$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Canonical form]
The \emph{canonical form} $\can(t)$ has $i$-th entry the least index $j$ with $t_j=t_i$. Two tuples have
the same kernel iff they have the same canonical form, and $\can$ is idempotent. A tuple $p$ with
$\can(p)=p$ is a \emph{pattern}; $\mathcal{P}_n$ denotes the set of patterns of length $n$, so that
$\#\mathcal{P}_n=B_n$ with $B_3=5$ and $B_4=15$.
\end{definition}

For $n=3$ we write $\pat{012}$, $\pat{002}$, $\pat{010}$, $\pat{011}$, $\pat{000}$ for the five
patterns: all distinct; $t_0=t_1\neq t_2$; $t_0=t_2\neq t_1$; $t_1=t_2\neq t_0$; all equal.

\begin{definition}[Kernel spectrum and defect]
For a set $S\subseteq\NN^n$ of tuples, $\Spec(S)=\{\can(t):t\in S\}\subseteq\mathcal{P}_n$ and
$\defect(S)=B_n-\#\Spec(S)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Conics, higher cones, refinement]
For $A,B,C\in\NN$ put $\mathcal{C}(A,B,C)=\{(x,y,z)\in\NN^3: Ax^2+By^2=Cz^2\}$, write
$\Spec(A,B,C)$ for its spectrum and $\defect(A,B,C)=5-\#\Spec(A,B,C)$. For $k\ge1$ put
$\mathcal{K}_k=\{(x_1,\dots,x_k,y)\in\NN^{k+1}:\sum_i x_i^2=y^2\}$, the \emph{legs} first and the
\emph{hypotenuse} last; a realised pattern is \emph{hypotenuse-merged} if the last coordinate shares a
block with a leg. For patterns $p,q$ write $p\preceq q$ (``$p$ refines $q$'') when $p_i=p_j\Rightarrow
q_i=q_j$; the symmetric group acts by $\sigma\cdot p=\can(p\circ\sigma)$.
\end{definition}

\section{The arithmetic engine}

\begin{theorem}[Descent, plain form]\label{thm:descent}
Let $P,Q\in\NN$ with $Q\neq0$. There exist $u,v\in\NN$ with $u\neq0$ and $Pu^2=Qv^2$ if and only if
$PQ$ is a perfect square.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) Multiply by $Q$: $PQ\,u^2=Q(Pu^2)=Q(Qv^2)=(Qv)^2$, and $k a^2=c^2$ with $a\neq0$ forces
$k$ to be a square. ($\Leftarrow$) If $PQ=m^2$, take $(u,v)=(Q,m)$, since $PQ^2=(PQ)Q=Qm^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Descent with non-degeneracy]\label{thm:descent2}
Let $P,Q\in\NN$ with $Q\neq0$. There exist $u,v\in\NN$ with $u\neq0$, $u\neq v$ and $Pu^2=Qv^2$ if and
only if $PQ$ is a perfect square and $P\neq Q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The square condition is Theorem~\ref{thm:descent}. If $P=Q$, cancelling $Q$ from $Qu^2=Qv^2$ gives
$u=v$. Conversely with $PQ=m^2$ take $(u,v)=(Q,m)$; were $Q=m$ then $PQ=Q^2$ and $P=Q$.
\end{proof}

The clause $P\neq Q$ is a genuinely different obstruction, not about the multiplicative structure of
$PQ$ but about the impossibility of separating $u$ from $v$. We call it the \emph{degeneracy
obstruction}.

\section{Complete criteria for ternary conics}\label{sec:conics}

\begin{proposition}
For all $A,B,C$ the pattern $\pat{000}$ lies in $\Spec(A,B,C)$, realised by the origin; hence
$\defect(A,B,C)\le4$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Equal legs]\label{thm:002}
Let $C\neq0$. Then $\pat{002}\in\Spec(A,B,C)$, i.e.\ there is a solution with $x=y\neq z$, if and only
if $(A+B)C$ is a perfect square and $A+B\neq C$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A solution $(x,x,z)$ with $x\neq z$ satisfies $(A+B)x^2=Cz^2$; if $x=0$ then $Cz^2=0$ and $C\neq0$
gives $z=0=x$, so $x\neq0$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:descent2} applies with $(P,Q,u,v)=(A+B,C,x,z)$.
Conversely the theorem produces $u\neq0$, $u\neq v$ with $(A+B)u^2=Cv^2$, and $(u,u,v)$ is the desired
solution.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[First leg meets the hypotenuse]\label{thm:010}
Let $B\neq0$. Then $\pat{010}\in\Spec(A,B,C)$ if and only if $A\le C$, $(C-A)B$ is a perfect square and
$A+B\neq C$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A solution $(x,y,x)$ satisfies $Ax^2+By^2=Cx^2$; as above $x\neq0$, whence $Ax^2\le Cx^2$ and $A\le C$,
and $(C-A)x^2=By^2$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:descent2} with $(P,Q,u,v)=(C-A,B,x,y)$; the clause
$C-A\neq B$ is exactly $A+B\neq C$. The converse produces the witness $(u,v,u)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Second leg meets the hypotenuse]\label{thm:011}
Let $A\neq0$. Then $\pat{011}\in\Spec(A,B,C)$ if and only if $B\le C$, $(C-B)A$ is a perfect square and
$A+B\neq C$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Diagonal degeneracy]\label{thm:diag}
Let $A,B,C\neq0$ with $A+B=C$, i.e.\ suppose $(1,1,1)$ lies on the conic. Then
$\Spec(A,B,C)\subseteq\{\pat{012},\pat{000}\}$: all three mixed patterns are blocked simultaneously and
$\defect(A,B,C)\ge3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each of Theorems~\ref{thm:002}--\ref{thm:011} requires $A+B\neq C$.
\end{proof}

A single linear condition on the coefficients annihilates three independent quadratic phenomena. The
prototype is $x^2+y^2=2z^2$, with infinitely many solutions ($1^2+7^2=2\cdot5^2$,
$7^2+17^2=2\cdot13^2$, \dots) but no partial coincidence among the coordinates of any of them.

\section{The defect is a surjective invariant}\label{sec:surj}

Fix $A=B=1$ and vary $C$, giving the pencil $\mathcal{E}_C: x^2+y^2=Cz^2$.

\begin{theorem}[$C=1$]
$\Spec(1,1,1)=\{\pat{012},\pat{010},\pat{011},\pat{000}\}$, so $\defect(1,1,1)=1$ and the unique missing
pattern is $\pat{002}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Witnesses $(3,4,5)$, $(7,0,7)$, $(0,7,7)$, $(0,0,0)$; and $\pat{002}$ would require $2$ to be a square
by Theorem~\ref{thm:002}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$C=50$: defect $0$]
$\Spec(1,1,50)=\mathcal{P}_3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$17^2+31^2=1250=50\cdot5^2$ is discrete; $(1+1)\cdot50=100=10^2$ with $2\neq50$ gives equal legs,
$5^2+5^2=50\cdot1^2$; $(50-1)\cdot1=49=7^2$ gives both leg--hypotenuse patterns, e.g.\
$1^2+7^2=50\cdot1^2$; the origin gives $\pat{000}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$C=8$: defect $2$]
$\Spec(1,1,8)=\{\pat{012},\pat{000},\pat{002}\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$2^2+14^2=200=8\cdot5^2$; $(1+1)\cdot8=16=4^2$ with $2\neq8$ gives $2^2+2^2=8\cdot1^2$; both
leg--hypotenuse patterns require $(8-1)\cdot1=7$ to be a square, which it is not.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$C=2$: defect $3$]
$\Spec(1,1,2)=\{\pat{012},\pat{000}\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Here $A+B=C$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:diag} blocks all mixed patterns; $(1,7,5)$ and $(0,0,0)$ realise the
survivors.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Descent at $3$]\label{thm:desc3}
If $x^2+y^2=3z^2$ with $x,y,z\in\NN$ then $x=y=z=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Squares are $0$ or $1$ modulo $3$, so $3\mid x^2+y^2$ forces $3\mid x$ and $3\mid y$. Writing $x=3a$,
$y=3b$ gives $3(a^2+b^2)=z^2$, hence $3\mid z$; writing $z=3c$ returns $a^2+b^2=3c^2$ with a strictly
smaller sum $a+b+c$ unless all vanish. Strong induction on $x+y+z$ concludes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$C=3$: defect $4$, maximal]
$\Spec(1,1,3)=\{\pat{000}\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Surjectivity of the defect]\label{thm:surj}
For each $d\in\{0,1,2,3,4\}$ there is a $C$ with $\defect(1,1,C)=d$, namely $C=50,1,8,2,3$. Since
$\defect(A,B,C)\le4$ always,
\[
\{\,d\in\NN:\exists C,\ \defect(1,1,C)=d\,\}=\{0,1,2,3,4\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

The defect is not constant along the pencil, is not monotone in $C$ (for $C=1,2,3,8,50$ it reads
$1,3,4,2,0$), and the Pythagorean value $1$ is neither extreme.

\section{Higher dimension: rigidity and constant legs}\label{sec:higher}

\begin{theorem}[Hypotenuse--leg rigidity]\label{thm:rigid}
If $x_1,\dots,x_k,y\in\NN$ satisfy $\sum_{i} x_i^2=y^2$ and $x_j=y$ for some $j$, then $x_i=0$ for all
$i\neq j$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\sum_{i\neq j}x_i^2+x_j^2=y^2=x_j^2$, so $\sum_{i\neq j}x_i^2=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
No realised pattern of $\mathcal{K}_k$ merges the hypotenuse with a leg while separating two other legs.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Dichotomy]
A solution of $\sum_{i<k}x_i^2=y^2$ whose hypotenuse equals some leg $x_j$ is either identically zero
or \emph{one-hot}: $x_j=y\neq0$ and $x_i=0$ for $i\neq j$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Constant-legs criterion, all dimensions]\label{thm:constlegs}
There exist $a,y\in\NN$ with $a\neq0$, $a\neq y$ and $\sum_{i=1}^{k}a^2=y^2$ if and only if $k$ is a
perfect square and $k\neq1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The equation is $ka^2=1\cdot y^2$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:descent2} with $P=k$, $Q=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The constant-legs pattern is blocked for $k=2$ and $k=3$ (as $2$ and $3$ are not squares), realised for
$k=4$ by $1^2+1^2+1^2+1^2=2^2$, and blocked for $k=1$ by the degeneracy clause alone.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The hypotenuse-merged part]\label{thm:merged}
For $k\ge2$ the hypotenuse-merged patterns of $\mathcal{K}_k$ are exactly the all-equal pattern together
with one pattern per leg, the pattern of the one-hot solution. Hence there are exactly $k+1$ of them
and $\#\Spec(\mathcal{K}_k)\ge k+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Spectrum of $x^2+y^2+z^2=w^2$]\label{thm:dim3}
The cone $\mathcal{K}_3$ realises exactly eight of the $B_4=15$ patterns of a quadruple, with witnesses
$(0,0,0,0)$, $(0,0,1,1)$, $(2,2,1,3)$, $(0,1,0,1)$, $(2,1,2,3)$, $(1,0,0,1)$, $(1,2,2,3)$, $(2,3,6,7)$.
Hence $\defect(\mathcal{K}_3)=7$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The witnesses give one inclusion. Conversely, enumerate the fifteen patterns: six of the seven
remaining merge the hypotenuse with a leg while separating two other legs and are excluded by
Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid}; the seventh is the constant-legs pattern, excluded by
Theorem~\ref{thm:constlegs} since $3$ is not a square.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The defect jumps from $1$ in dimension two to $7$ in dimension three, although the number of realised
patterns merely doubles while the number of available patterns triples.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Equivariance and orbit decomposition]
Permuting coordinates commutes with canonicalisation, so the spectrum of an equation invariant under a
group of coordinate permutations is a union of orbits. For $\mathcal{K}_3$ the seven missing patterns
split under the leg-permutation group into orbits of sizes $1+3+3$: the fixed constant-legs pattern,
the orbit of ``hypotenuse merged with two legs'', and the orbit of ``hypotenuse merged with exactly one
leg, other legs distinct''.
\end{theorem}

This mirrors the proof structure: the size-one orbit matches the symmetric obstruction ($3$ is not a
square), the size-three orbits the asymmetric rigidity obstruction.

\section{Higher exponents: the Fermat pencil}\label{sec:fermat}

\begin{theorem}[$p$-th power descent]
If $ka^p=c^p$ with $a\neq0$, then $k$ is a $p$-th power.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put $g=\gcd(a,c)$, $a=ga'$, $c=gc'$ with $\gcd(a',c')=1$; cancelling $g^p$ gives $ka'^p=c'^p$, so
$a'^p\mid c'^p$ and coprimality forces $a'=1$, whence $k=c'^p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Descent with non-degeneracy, exponent $p$]
For $p\ge1$ and $Q\neq0$, the equation $Pu^p=Qv^p$ has a solution with $u\neq0$, $u\neq v$ if and only
if $PQ^{p-1}$ is a $p$-th power and $P\neq Q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Equal legs for $Ax^p+By^p=Cz^p$]\label{thm:fermatlegs}
For $p\ge1$ and $C\neq0$, the pencil has a solution with $x=y\neq z$ if and only if $(A+B)C^{p-1}$ is a
$p$-th power and $A+B\neq C$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
For every $p\ge2$ the equation $x^p+y^p=z^p$ has no solution with $x=y\neq z$, since the criterion would
require $2$ to be a $p$-th power. This is elementary and independent of Fermat's Last Theorem.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Cubic trichotomy]\label{thm:trich}
At the exponent $p=3$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $x^3+y^3=z^3$ has no equal-legs solution, blocked by the power obstruction ($2$ is not a cube);
\item $x^3+y^3=2z^3$ has no equal-legs solution, blocked by the degeneracy obstruction alone, since the
power condition $2\cdot2^2=8=2^3$ does hold while $1+1=2=C$;
\item $x^3+y^3=16z^3$ does have one, namely $2^3+2^3=16\cdot1^3$; here $2\cdot16^2=512=8^3$ and
$2\neq16$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular the two obstructions are logically independent, and the blocking of the isosceles pattern
for Fermat's equation is a fact about the coefficient rather than the exponent.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Defect of the Fermat family]
For $p\ge3$, $\Spec(x^p+y^p=z^p)=\{\pat{000},\pat{010},\pat{011}\}$ and the defect is $2$; for $p=2$ the
defect is $1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The origin and the solutions $(a,0,a)$, $(0,a,a)$ give the three listed patterns; $\pat{002}$ is blocked
by the corollary above; and $\pat{012}$ requires three pairwise distinct coordinates, necessarily all
positive, that is a counterexample to Fermat's Last Theorem, which does not exist. For $p=2$ the
pattern $\pat{012}$ is realised by $(3,4,5)$.
\end{proof}

Thus ``the spectrum of $x^p+y^p=z^p$ has exactly three elements'' is equivalent to Fermat's Last
Theorem at exponent $p$: a deep theorem reformulated as the value of a finite invariant.

\section{Order-theoretic remarks}

\begin{theorem}[Non-convexity]
The Pythagorean spectrum is not order-convex: along the chain
$\pat{012}\prec\pat{002}\prec\pat{000}$ it contains both endpoints but not the middle term. In
particular it is neither an order filter nor an order ideal. The same failure occurs in dimension
three along $\pat{0123}\prec\pat{0003}\prec\pat{0000}$.
\end{theorem}

The defect is therefore an \emph{interior} phenomenon, invisible at the extremes of the partition
lattice where realisability is either trivial (the origin) or generic. No monotone or lattice-theoretic
shortcut can compute spectra; each pattern carries its own arithmetic.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Spectrum of a ternary conic by criteria.}
Given $A,B,C>0$ and a search bound $N$: mark $\pat{000}$ realised; mark $\pat{002}$ realised iff
$(A+B)C$ is a square and $A+B\neq C$; mark $\pat{010}$ realised iff $A\le C$, $(C-A)B$ is a square and
$A+B\neq C$; mark $\pat{011}$ symmetrically; mark $\pat{012}$ realised iff a pairwise-distinct solution
with coordinates at most $N$ is found; output the marked set and $5$ minus its size. Steps for the
mixed patterns are exact and cost $O(\log C)$ integer operations each (one integer square root); the
discrete step is a bounded $O(N^2)$ search and is the only heuristic ingredient, decisive positively
and certifiable negatively only by a descent as in Theorem~\ref{thm:desc3}.

\paragraph{Brute-force enumeration.}
Enumerate triples with entries at most $N$, canonicalise the solutions, and collect patterns. Sound but
not complete: absence may reflect a large witness. On the pencil $x^2+y^2=Cz^2$ the two procedures
agree for modest bounds in every case treated above, including $C=3$, where the criteria (backed by
descent) prove what enumeration can only suggest.

\paragraph{Constant-legs test.}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:constlegs} the constant-legs question for $\sum_{i<k}x_i^2=y^2$ is the single
predicate ``$k$ is a perfect square and $k\neq1$'', an $O(\log k)$ computation: an infinite family of
Diophantine questions collapsing to one arithmetic test.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

The spectrum is a bounded amount of data ($B_n$ bits) extracted from a possibly infinite solution set,
invariant under scaling and under the coordinate symmetries of the equation. Each blocked pattern
encodes a classical theorem: equal legs on $x^2+y^2=z^2$ is the irrationality of $\sqrt2$; the collapse
of $x^2+y^2=2z^2$ is diagonal degeneracy; the collapse of $x^2+y^2=3z^2$ is descent at $3$; constant
legs on $x^2+y^2+z^2=w^2$ is the non-squareness of $3$; equal legs on $x^p+y^p=z^p$ is the
non-$p$-th-power-ness of $2$; and the discrete pattern on $x^p+y^p=z^p$ for $p\ge3$ is Fermat's Last
Theorem. Because the criteria involve the three independent products $(A+B)C$, $(C-A)B$, $(C-B)A$, one
may hope to \emph{design} conics with prescribed spectra by choosing squarefree parts, subject to the
coupling imposed by the common clause $A+B\neq C$; Theorem~\ref{thm:surj} is the two-dimensional
confirmation that nothing hidden prevents this.

\section{Discussion}

Three themes emerge. First, the square and degeneracy obstructions are of different natures -- one
multiplicative and arithmetic, one linear and geometric -- and the cubic trichotomy
(Theorem~\ref{thm:trich}) shows them logically independent; in the Pythagorean case the degeneracy
clause is invisible because $1+1\neq1$, which is why classical treatments never notice it. Second,
rigidity is dimension-free while squareness is not: Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid} holds for all $k$ with a
one-line proof and produces the bulk of the higher-dimensional defect, leaving exactly one
dimension-sensitive bit, pinned down by Theorem~\ref{thm:constlegs}. Third, the invariant is fine
enough to separate the five members $C=50,1,8,2,3$ of a single pencil and to detect Fermat's Last
Theorem, yet coarse enough to be a small integer computable by explicit criteria.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Dimension formula]
For every $k\ge2$,
\[
\#\Spec\Big(\textstyle\sum_{i<k}x_i^2=y^2\Big)=(k+1)+\big(B_k-[\,k\text{ is not a perfect square}\,]\big).
\]
Equivalently, every partition of the legs is realisable with the hypotenuse in a block of its own,
except the all-legs-equal partition when $k$ is not a square.
\end{conjecture}

The two obstruction theorems proved here are plausibly exhaustive: rigidity pins the merged part to
exactly $k+1$ patterns (Theorem~\ref{thm:merged}), and the constant-legs criterion is the only
obstruction remaining once the legs take at least two distinct values, since with two free values one
can already solve $n_1a^2+n_2b^2=y^2$ by a Pythagorean parametrisation. The formula is verified for
$k=1,2,3$ by proof (giving $1,4,8$) and for $k=4$ by enumeration ($5+15=20$, with $B_4=15$ and $4$ a
square). The missing step is a single existence lemma: for block sizes $n_1,\dots,n_r$ with $r\ge2$
there are distinct $a_1,\dots,a_r$ and $y\notin\{a_i\}$ with $\sum n_ia_i^2=y^2$.

\begin{conjecture}[Defect surjectivity in every dimension]
For every $k\ge2$ and every $d\le B_{k+1}-1$ there is a positive-definite diagonal cone
$\sum_i A_ix_i^2=Cy^2$ whose defect equals $d$.
\end{conjecture}

The conic pencil already realises all five possible defects in dimension two, and the criteria show
that each pattern is switched on or off by an independent arithmetic condition of the shape
``$(C-A_i)A_j$ is a square'', so the coefficients should be tunable one pattern at a time via the
Chinese Remainder Theorem applied to squarefree parts.

\begin{conjecture}[Invariance properties]
Two ternary conics isomorphic over $\QQ$ can have different defects; but the condition $A+B=C$
(equivalently, that $(1,1,1)$ is a rational point) is invariant under monomial transformations and
alone controls the simultaneous vanishing of all three mixed patterns.
\end{conjecture}

Further directions: extend the criteria to indefinite ternary forms, where the Hasse--Minkowski
local--global principle should render the discrete pattern effectively decidable, removing the only
heuristic step in the algorithm; classify which subsets of $\mathcal{P}_3$ arise as spectra of ternary
conics; and study the analogous invariant for inhomogeneous or non-diagonal equations, where the
interaction between mixed terms and equality patterns is genuinely new.

\section{Conclusion}

The kernel spectrum turns a qualitative question -- which coincidences among coordinates a solution can
exhibit -- into a finite, computable invariant. For ternary conics we determined it completely: three
closed-form criteria, each a square condition paired with a common degeneracy clause; a structural
corollary showing that a rational diagonal point annihilates all mixed patterns at once; and a proof
that along the pencil $x^2+y^2=Cz^2$ the defect attains every one of its five possible values. In
higher dimension a single rigidity theorem dictates the bulk of the answer, leaving one
dimension-sensitive bit governed by the criterion ``the leg count is a perfect square other than
$1$''. At higher exponents the machinery runs with $p$-th powers, and the two obstructions separate
cleanly at the cubic exponent. The Pythagorean cone sits at the centre as the smallest interesting
example: defect $1$, one missing pattern, and behind it the oldest irrationality proof in mathematics.

